        "findings": [
          {
            "source_quote": "ὡς ζηλοῦν αὐτοῦ μέλλοντος ἐν τοῖς ἐσομένοις ἢ γυναῖκα ἢ κτήματα ἢ τὴν τῶν γονέων πρὸς αὐτὸν στοργήν",
            "english_quote": "since in what was to come he was destined to be jealous of either a wife or possessions or the parents’ affection toward him",
            "issue": "The causal rendering is defensible, but the note discusses the pronouns without disclosing the material alternative supplied by ὡς with the genitive absolute: the anticipated jealousy can be presented as the namer’s assumed reason rather than an independently endorsed prediction. The combination of “since” and “was destined” favors the latter impression. The following conditional, “εἰ δ’ ἄρα μηδὲν τούτων γένηται,” makes this distinction especially relevant.",
            "suggested_revision": "Add a note preserving the alternative “on the assumption that in the future he would be jealous of either a wife or possessions or the parents’ affection toward him,” explaining the attributed-ground force available to ὡς and the following contingency. Explicitly compare that wording with the causal main rendering. Consider using “was going to” instead of “was destined to” to avoid suggesting necessary fulfillment. Any changed main text requires fresh review.",
            "severity": "minor"
          }
        ]
      }
